+# CLI Contract: `python -m bookmarks`
+
+**Feature**: `001-bookmark-manager-cli` | **Date**: 2026-11-20
+
+This is the feature's external interface — the contract a user or a calling script may rely
+on. It is the authority for the command surface, the exact output bytes, and the exit status
+of every path. Entity fields and the storage schema are in [data-model.md](../data-model.md);
+design rationale is in [research.md](../research.md).
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m bookmarks [--db PATH] COMMAND [ARGS...]
+```
+
+`src/` must be on `PYTHONPATH` (FR-001, spec Technical Constraints). `--db` is a global
+option and must precede the command.
+
+| Option | Default | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `--db PATH` | `bookmarks.db` in the current working directory | The collection's database file. Created with mode `0600` if absent (FR-002, FR-003, SR-005). |
+
+Exactly five commands exist. There is no `update`, `import`, `tag-rename`, interactive mode,
+colour option, pagination or configuration file (spec Scope boundaries).
+
+## Commands
+
+### `add URL [--title TITLE] [--tags TAGS]`
+
+Stores one bookmark.
+
+| Element | Contract |
+|---|---|
+| `URL` | Positional, required. Accepted only if it begins with the exact lowercase `http://` or `https://` (FR-008). Max 8192 chars (SR-006). |
+| `--title TITLE` | Optional, defaults to `""` (FR-009). Max 4096 chars (SR-006). |
+| `--tags TAGS` | Optional comma-separated list, e.g. `python,cli`; defaults to no tags (FR-010). Parsed per FR-012. Max 4096 chars, ≤64 tags, each ≤128 chars (SR-006). |
+| stdout | The new identifier as a decimal integer followed by a newline — **and nothing else** (FR-006). |
+| stderr | Empty on success. |
+| Status | 0 on success; 2 for an invalid URL or any oversized input; 1 if the database is unusable. |
+
+```console
+$ python -m bookmarks --db b.db add https://docs.python.org --title "Python docs" --tags "python,docs"
+1
+```
+
+### `list [--tag TAG]`
+
+Prints stored bookmarks in the listing format below.
+
+| Element | Contract |
+|---|---|
+| `--tag TAG` | Optional. Prints only bookmarks carrying `TAG`, matched as an exact, case-sensitive string (FR-014). A matched bookmark still displays **all** of its tags. |
+| stdout | Zero or more listing lines, ascending identifier order (FR-013, FR-017). |
+| stderr | Empty. |
+| Status | 0 — including when nothing matches (FR-018); 1 if the database is unusable. |
+
+### `search TEXT`
+
+| Element | Contract |
+|---|---|
+| `TEXT` | Positional, required. Matched as a **case-insensitive literal substring** against title and URL (FR-015). `%` and `_` match only themselves (FR-016, SR-004). Max 1024 chars (SR-006). |
+| stdout | Zero or more listing lines, ascending identifier order, same format as `list`. |
+| stderr | Empty. |
+| Status | 0 — including no matches (FR-018); 2 if `TEXT` is oversized; 1 if the database is unusable. |
+
+### `delete ID`
+
+| Element | Contract |
+|---|---|
+| `ID` | Positional, required. A decimal positive integer, 1 … 2^63−1 (FR-022, SR-003). |
+| stdout | Empty. |
+| stderr | Empty on success; one line otherwise. |
+| Status | 0 on removal; **3** if no bookmark has that identifier (FR-021); **2** if `ID` is malformed (`abc`, `1.5`, `-1`, `0`, absent); 1 if the database is unusable. |
+
+Removal takes the bookmark's tag associations with it and leaves every other bookmark, its
+identifier, title and tags untouched (FR-019, FR-020). The identifier is never reissued
+(FR-007).
+
+### `export --format {csv,json}`
+
+| Element | Contract |
+|---|---|
+| `--format` | **Required**; only `csv` or `json` (FR-026). |
+| stdout | The whole collection in the chosen format, ascending identifier order. |
+| stderr | Empty on success. |
+| Status | 0; 2 for a missing or unsupported `--format`; 1 if the database is unusable. |
+
+## Output formats
+
+All output is UTF-8 with `\n` line endings (spec Assumptions).
+
+### Listing format (`list`, `search`)
+
+One bookmark per line, exactly four tab-separated fields, no header, no trailing blank line
+(FR-017):
+
+```text
+{id}\t{url}\t{title}\t{tags}
+```
+
+- `tags` is the bookmark's tags joined with `,` in ascending code-point order — so uppercase
+  before lowercase (`Docs,python`).
+- An absent title and an absent tag set render as empty fields; a bookmark with neither yields
+  a line ending in two consecutive tabs.
+- Tabs and newlines inside a title are reproduced verbatim, with no escaping, which makes that
+  one line ambiguous to split — accepted by the spec's Assumptions.
+
+```text
+1→https://docs.python.org→Python docs→docs,python
+2→https://example.com→→
+```
+
+(`→` shown for the tab character.)
+
+### CSV format (`export --format csv`)
+
+- First line is exactly `id,url,title,tags` (FR-023).
+- One row per bookmark after it, ascending identifier order.
+- `tags` is a single field holding the comma-joined, code-point-sorted tags — quoted by the
+  writer whenever it contains a comma, which it does whenever a bookmark has two or more tags.
+- RFC 4180 quoting via Python's `csv` module: a field containing `,`, `"` or a newline is
+  double-quoted and inner `"` is doubled, so a title round-trips unchanged through any
+  standard CSV parser (FR-025).
+- Line terminator is `\n`.
+- An empty collection produces the header line and nothing else (FR-027).
+- Fields are **not** spreadsheet-hardened: a leading `=`, `+`, `-` or `@` in a title is
+  emitted as-is, because FR-025 requires exact round-tripping. Documented deviation — see
+  [plan.md](../plan.md#complexity-tracking).
+
+```csv
+id,url,title,tags
+1,https://docs.python.org,Python docs,"docs,python"
+2,https://example.com,,
+```
+
+### JSON format (`export --format json`)
+
+- A single JSON array, ascending identifier order (FR-024).
+- One object per bookmark with exactly the keys `id`, `url`, `title`, `tags`.
+- `id` is a JSON **number**; `url` and `title` are strings; `tags` is an **array** of strings
+  in ascending code-point order (an empty array when there are none).
+- Non-ASCII characters are emitted as literal UTF-8 (`ensure_ascii=False`), which is valid
+  JSON; quotes, newlines and control characters are escaped by the JSON encoder, so a title
+  round-trips unchanged (FR-025).
+- An empty collection produces `[]` (FR-027).
+
+```json
+[{"id": 1, "url": "https://docs.python.org", "title": "Python docs", "tags": ["docs", "python"]},
+ {"id": 2, "url": "https://example.com", "title": "", "tags": []}]
+```
+
+(Whitespace between elements is not part of the contract; the parsed value is.)
+
+## Exit statuses and error contract
+
+Every error writes a single-line, human-readable message naming what was wrong to **standard
+error**, writes nothing of it to standard output, and exits non-zero (FR-005). No expected
+failure prints a traceback, and no message contains SQL text (SR-008).
+
+| Status | Meaning | Cases |
+|---|---|---|
+| **0** | Success | Every command that completed, including a `list`/`search` that matched nothing and an `export` of an empty collection (FR-004, FR-018, FR-027). |
+| **1** | Any other failure | `--db` parent directory does not exist; database file unreadable, unwritable, or not a valid SQLite database; identifier sequence exhausted. Never a silent fallback to the default path and never an empty collection (spec Edge Cases). |
+| **2** | Invalid argument or input | Unknown command; unknown option; missing required argument; invalid URL (FR-008); malformed `delete ID` (FR-022); missing or unsupported `export --format` (FR-026); any input over its SR-006 limit. |
+| **3** | Unknown identifier | `delete ID` where `ID` is well-formed but no bookmark has it (FR-021). |
+
+**Validation order** (fixed, and asserted by tests): argparse-level parsing → size limits
+(SR-006) → URL shape (FR-008) → database access. Every exit-2 case is therefore decided
+before the database is opened, so a rejected command performs no read and no write
+(SR-009, spec Edge Cases).
+
+**Status 2 vs status 3** is the distinction between a *malformed* identifier and a *well-formed
+but absent* one: `delete abc` → 2, `delete 0` → 2, `delete 999` on a collection without 999 →
+3.
+
+## Guarantees
+
+| Guarantee | Requirement |
+|---|---|
+| Identifiers start at 1, strictly increase, and are never reused after deletion | FR-007 |
+| Every write is atomic — a bookmark and its tags commit together or not at all | SR-009 |
+| State persists across process exit for the same `--db` path; two paths never interact | FR-003, spec Key Entities |
+| Every statement is parameterised; user text is never interpolated into SQL | SR-001 |
+| No network access, no subprocess, no shell, no environment-driven configuration | FR-011, SR-010 |
+| The database file is created mode `0600`; no temporary file holding bookmark data survives a command | SR-005, SR-011 |
+| `list`, `search` and `export` stream rows — output memory does not grow with collection size | SR-007 |
